---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Docker">
  <h1>Running with Docker</h1>
  <p>
    The official image is <code>ghcr.io/elloloop/identity</code>. It is a
    multi-arch (linux/amd64, linux/arm64) scratch-based static image with the
    statically-linked <code>/bin/identity</code> binary as ENTRYPOINT.
  </p>

  <h2>Pull a release</h2>
  <Code code={`docker pull ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0
# Or always-latest (not recommended for prod):
docker pull ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:latest`} lang="bash" />
  <p>Tag formats produced by the release workflow:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>1.2.0</code> — exact version</li>
    <li><code>1.2</code>, <code>1</code> — major/minor floats</li>
    <li><code>latest</code> — non-prerelease tags only</li>
    <li><code>sha-&lt;commit&gt;</code> — commit SHA pin</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Minimal run</h2>
  <Code code={`docker run --rm -p 80:80 -p 9090:9090 \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=sqlite \\
  -e GATEWAY_SQLITE_PATH=:memory: \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_local \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0`} lang="bash" title="dev (embedded sqlite, auto-generated JWT key)" />
  <p>
    With no <code>GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE</code> set, the container generates an
    ephemeral RSA key and logs <code>jwt_signer_dev_key_generated</code>.
    Restarting the container produces a new key — every existing access
    token becomes unverifiable. Use this for local dev only.
  </p>

  <h2>Production run</h2>
  <Code code={`docker run -d --restart=always \\
  --name identity \\
  -p 80:80 -p 9090:9090 \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://identity:secret@postgres.svc:5432/identity \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_acme \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID=acme.example.com \\
  -e GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN=https://acme.example.com \\
  -e GATEWAY_TOTP_ISSUER="Acme" \\
  -e GATEWAY_TOTP_ENCRYPTION_KEY="$(openssl rand -base64 32)" \\
  -e GATEWAY_PROJECT_SECRETS_KEY="$(openssl rand -base64 32)" \\
  -v /etc/identity/keys.json:/etc/identity/keys.json:ro \\
  -e GATEWAY_JWT_SIGNER=file \\
  -e GATEWAY_JWT_KEYS_FILE=/etc/identity/keys.json \\
  -e GATEWAY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS="https://acme.example.com" \\
  -e GATEWAY_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=... \\
  -e GATEWAY_OAUTH_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=... \\
  -e GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL=true \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0`} lang="bash" title="production" />

  <h2>Bootstrap the first platform admin before exposing it</h2>
  <p>
    On a Postgres deployment the control plane exposes
    <code>CreateFirstPlatformAdmin</code> — a one-time, trust-on-first-use
    bootstrap that creates the <strong>first</strong> platform operator while
    the <code>platform_admins</code> table is empty, then permanently closes
    once any admin exists. <strong>Run it before you expose the container to the
    public internet</strong>: on a fresh, internet-reachable deployment an
    anonymous caller could otherwise win the first-admin race.
  </p>
  <Code code={`curl -sX POST http://localhost/identity.v1.IdentityService/CreateFirstPlatformAdmin \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"email":"ops@acme.example.com"}'
# → { "adminId": "...", "email": "ops@acme.example.com", "generatedPassword": "..." }`} lang="bash" title="one-time first-admin bootstrap" />
  <p>Pick whichever fits your deploy pipeline:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Bootstrap before exposure.</strong> Create the first admin over a
      private network or an SSH port-forward, then open the service to traffic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Gate the bootstrap on the admin secret.</strong> When
      <code>GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET</code> is set, this RPC requires the same
      <code>X-Admin-Secret</code> header as the other admin RPCs, so it is no
      longer anonymous. The bootstrap stays zero-config only when no secret is
      configured, so a deployment already running with an admin secret is
      protected by default.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Close the surface entirely.</strong> Set
      <code>GATEWAY_DISABLE_FIRST_ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP=true</code>; the RPC then
      returns <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> regardless of whether any admin
      exists. With it closed, create the first admin by a direct insert into
      <code>platform_admins</code>, or toggle the flag off just long enough to
      bootstrap and back on — no first-party seed CLI ships.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Building from source</h2>
  <p>The repo's <code>Dockerfile</code> is a two-stage multi-arch build:</p>
  <Code code={`FROM --platform=$BUILDPLATFORM golang:1.26.5-alpine3.23 AS builder

ARG TARGETOS
ARG TARGETARCH
ARG VERSION=dev
ARG COMMIT=unknown

WORKDIR /app
COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download
COPY cmd/    ./cmd/
COPY identityserver/ ./identityserver/
COPY internal/ ./internal/
COPY pkg/    ./pkg/
COPY gen/    ./gen/

RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=$TARGETOS GOARCH=$TARGETARCH \\
    go build \\
      -ldflags="-s -w -X main.version=$VERSION -X main.commit=$COMMIT" \\
      -o /bin/identity \\
      ./cmd/identity

FROM scratch AS server
COPY --from=builder /bin/identity /bin/identity
COPY --from=builder /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
EXPOSE 80 9090
USER 65532:65532
ENTRYPOINT ["/bin/identity"]`} lang="dockerfile" title="Dockerfile" />
  <Code code={`docker build -t identity:dev .
docker run --rm -p 80:80 identity:dev`} lang="bash" />

  <h2>docker-compose</h2>
  <Code code={`version: "3.8"

services:
  postgres:
    image: postgres:16.4
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: identity
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: secret
      POSTGRES_DB: identity
    ports: ["5432:5432"]

  identity:
    image: ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0
    depends_on: [postgres]
    ports:
      - "80:80"
      - "9090:9090"
    environment:
      GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER: postgres
      GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN: postgres://identity:secret@postgres:5432/identity?sslmode=disable
      GATEWAY_POSTGRES_AUTO_MIGRATE: "true"
      GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID: proj_local
      # Required with postgres — encrypts per-project OAuth secrets at rest.
      # Dev-only all-zero key; generate a real one: openssl rand -base64 32
      GATEWAY_PROJECT_SECRETS_KEY: AAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAA=
      GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL: "true"
      GATEWAY_PASSKEY_RP_ID: localhost
      GATEWAY_PASSKEY_ORIGIN: http://localhost`} lang="yaml" title="docker-compose.yml" />

  <h2>Health check</h2>
  <Code code={`curl -sf http://localhost/health
# 200 OK`} lang="bash" />
</DocsLayout>
